    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d685">

                <pb facs="1138" n="1120" sameAs="frus:frus1948v03/pg1120"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.00/9–1348: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Embassy in the United
                        Kingdom </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-09-13T19:00:00-04:00">September 13,
                            1948—7 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3626. Embtel 4045 Sept 9.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d685-f1"/> During Jebb’s<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d685-f2"/> visit he stated UK only govt really doing concrete
                    planning looking toward Western European <pb facs="1139" n="1121"
                        xml:id="pg1121"/> integration at least in economic field. He was advised we
                    had seen very little evidence of it and that general impression here including
                    press and public was that UK dragging feet. We indicated this impression might
                    well be reflected in 1949 Congressional action on ECA unless Brit clearly showed
                    drive and concrete results achieved.</p>
                <p>Pls keep us informed developments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Marshall</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d685-f1" n="1" xml:id="d685-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the British Foreign Office favored consideration by
                    the five-power commission as the best approach to the question of a European
                    Assembly (840.00/9–949).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d685-f2" n="2" xml:id="d685-f2-content">
                <p>Hubert Miles Gladwyn Jebb, British representative on the Brussels Treaty
                    Permanent Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
